{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004155",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "There are many direct-match prostate cancer PRSs in context; the strongest-supported choice comes from a coherent, modern comparative family with consistent endpoint and large validation. PGS004155 (UKBB-EUR.MultiPRS.CV) targets prostate cancer directly (phenotyping_reported: \"Prostate cancer\"), uses no added clinical covariates (covariates: \"0\", which the domain guidance treats as closer to PRS-only than risk-wrapper/clinical packaging), and has large validation support (n=171,474; EUR-dominant evaluation). Within the same 2023 Am J Hum Genet multi-method family (PGS003985, PGS004001, PGS004027, PGS004042, PGS004139, PGS004155, etc.), it has the best reported discrimination (full_model_auc ~0.705) and variance explained (R2 ~0.128), making it the best-supported sibling under comparable endpoints and covariate settings. Key limitation: the reported AUROC is a full-model metric (not explicitly labeled PGS-only), and several other direct-match candidates report very high full-model AUROC under different endpoints or potential packaging (e.g., lethal/5-year risk/age-specific absolute-risk adjusted models), so cross-study metric comparability remains imperfect; this keeps confidence at Moderate despite strong within-family evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002036",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present for depressive disorder / major depressive disorder. PGS002036 is preferred because it is explicitly for depressive disorder (trait_efo) with phenotype reported as “Depression”, uses a modern genome-wide method (LDpred2), and has a reported PRS-comparable metric (partial correlation 0.0556, with CI) in European validation. However, the evidence remains sub-optimal because multiple close competitors exist with similarly aligned endpoints (e.g., PGS001829 from the same portability paper; several MDD-focused PRS-CS/PRS-CS-auto models such as PGS003333 and PGS002759; and multiple UKB-derived MDD variants), and key performance reporting is heterogeneous/non-comparable across models (many provide only full-model metrics or effect sizes, and some candidates have endpoint/context mismatches such as antidepressant side-effect phenotypes). This prevents a clearly best-supported, conflict-free selection from the visible metadata alone."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS000452","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_reported/trait_efo = basal cell carcinoma). For generic basal cell carcinoma risk, most candidates also have endpoint-faithful phenotyping_reported ('Basal cell carcinoma'), except PGS003416/PGS003417 (phenotyping_reported: 'Keratinocyte cancers', broader composite) and PGS005207 (restricted survivor endpoint: 'Risk of basal cell carcinoma in childhood cancer survivors'), which are down-ranked on endpoint fidelity per the provided policy.

Within the large coherent Cancer PRSweb family (PGS000445–PGS000459; same journal/title/date; EUR dev/eval; similar covariates limited to age/sex/batch/PCs), PGS000452 stands out with the strongest reported discrimination/effect package among the MGI-evaluated siblings: full-model AUROC 0.632 (0.616–0.647) plus the highest reported Nagelkerke pseudo-R² (0.0487) and strong OR per SD 1.663 (1.57–1.761), with consistent top-1% vs rest OR 3.61. Close siblings PGS000454 have essentially identical AUROC/R²/OR (0.632; R² 0.0489; OR 1.661) and PGS000456/PGS000458 are slightly lower (AUROC 0.630/0.628). Given the near-tie, selecting PGS000452 is based on it being part of the strongest-performing coherent endpoint-faithful cluster with clean covariates, rather than on a single outlier metric.

Main limitation: metrics are largely full-model AUROCs (not explicitly PRS-only), and the top candidates are extremely close (especially PGS000452 vs PGS000454), so the preference is modest and confidence is not high."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000461",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates for squamous cell carcinoma are present (e.g., PGS000120, PGS000460–PGS000468, PGS000731, PGS005209). Among the clean generic SCC endpoints (phenotyping_reported = \"Squamous cell carcinoma\" with basic age/sex/PC covariates), PGS000461 shows the strongest visible validation performance package in this context (full_model AUROC 0.599 with CI 0.579–0.618; Nagelkerke R² 0.0304; OR per SD 1.491; top-1% vs rest OR 3.51) compared with closely related siblings such as PGS000463 (AUROC 0.593) and PGS000120 (AUROC 0.59). However, confidence is limited because (1) only full-model AUROC/R² are reported (no explicit PGS-only AUROC/R²), so cross-model comparability is imperfect per the provided policy, and (2) a competing direct-match model PGS000731 reports slightly higher AUROC (0.605) but has unknown covariates and missing training information, making it hard to judge comparability; thus the best choice remains somewhat uncertain."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target concept “uterine cancer” are present, including exact/near-exact uterus endpoints (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735-002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Per the provided domain knowledge, endometrial carcinoma is the dominant histology for uterine carcinoma and is therefore a strong direct match.

PGS003381 is selected because it has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: “uterine endometrial carcinoma”; trait_efo: endometrial carcinoma) and comparatively strong reported discrimination among the endometrial/uterine-aligned set (full-model AUROC=0.761; R²=0.11) with only basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs) and large European validation (n=144,479). In contrast, the exact-label uterine cancer models from Cancer PRSweb (PGS000541/PGS000542) show notably lower AUROC (~0.57) in a much smaller validation (n=6,987), and PGS001795 (exact “Uterine cancer”) comes from a global biobank framework context with modest reported PRS-only R² (0.001948) and AUROC=0.66.

Main limitation: comparability of performance is imperfect because PGS003381 reports full-model AUROC/R² (not explicitly PRS-only), and several other candidates also lack clean PRS-only metrics; additionally, there is residual ambiguity across ‘uterine cancer’ umbrella vs ‘corpus uteri’/‘malignant neoplasm of uterus’ definitions. These factors prevent a high-confidence, clearly best-supported direct-match choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002027","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates exist for retinopathy primarily as diabetic retinopathy (PGS000819, PGS001819, PGS002027; plus PGS000862 but with phenotyping reported as “Moderate Age-Related Diabetes,” making endpoint alignment unclear). Several other returned models are age-related macular degeneration, which is not the target trait and is not a direct retinopathy endpoint match.

Among the diabetic retinopathy models, PGS002027 has strong endpoint fidelity (“Diabetic retinopathy” in trait_reported and phenotyping_reported), comes from the coherent UK Biobank portability family, and has the strongest available PRS-comparable metric among that family in the provided metadata (partial correlation 0.0451 vs 0.0366 for PGS001819). PGS000819 is disease-focused (type 2 diabetes retinopathy study) and reports an OR=1.12, but provides no PRS-comparable AUROC/R2 and has unknown covariates, making it hard to judge PRS contribution against the portability-family metrics.

Limitations: the target trait is generic “retinopathy,” while all endpoint-faithful options are specifically diabetic retinopathy (so the match is not perfectly generic), and most candidates lack directly comparable PRS-only AUROC/R2; the selection is therefore based on limited, heterogeneous performance reporting (partial-r vs OR) and study archetype differences."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002729","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to knee osteoarthritis via trait_efo="osteoarthritis, knee" (or a very close knee-OA endpoint). For knee-OA targets, domain guidance prefers site-specific OA diagnosis/incident OA over proxies like total knee replacement, broad OA labels, or pan-trait framework scores, and warns that BMI-adjusted full-model AUROC should not be treated as PRS-only evidence.

PGS002729 is preferred because it is explicitly disease-focused (publication: "Risk assessment for hip and knee osteoarthritis using polygenic risk scores"), uses a direct clinical osteoarthritis endpoint (phenotyping_reported: "Clinical osteoarthritis"), and is developed across a large multi-cohort set (BBJ, EB, GARP, HUNT, LLS, MyCode, UKB, arcOGEN, deCODE), which is supportive versus a pan-trait UKB framework score (PGS001192, snpnet "813 traits" paper) even though that framework score reports PRS-only AUROC/R2. Competitors are weaker on endpoint specificity (PGS002749 is total knee replacement), have limited performance reporting (PGS002767/PGS004479/PGS004549 mainly report ORs without AUROC/R2), or come from framework/risk-integration contexts.

Main limitation: the only discrimination metric shown for PGS002729 is a full-model AUROC=0.66 with covariates including BMI (a meaningful OA mediator), and PRS-only metrics are absent, reducing comparability and making it unclear how much of the AUROC is PRS-driven. This keeps confidence moderate and the overall outcome sub-optimal despite being the best-supported direct-match package in the provided metadata."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002738","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct/near-direct matches to the target concept (alcohol dependence): PGS002738 and PGS002739 are explicitly aligned to trait_efo="alcohol dependence" with phenotyping_reported="Alcohol use disorder"; PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled "Problematic alcohol use" but their phenotyping_reported is also AUD DSM-5 criteria count (log-transformed), making them endpoint-faithful.

PGS002738 is best supported on the visible evidence because it matches the target EFO directly and provides an interpretable disease-risk effect metric (OR top 10% vs rest = 1.96, 95% CI 1.54–2.51) in a reasonably sized European evaluation (n=7,900). It is also developed from multi-cohort sources (MVP, UKB), which is supportive versus the UKB-only development of PGS000201/PGS000202.

However, the choice is sub-optimal rather than high-quality because performance reporting is limited and not fully comparable across candidates: none report PRS-only AUROC/R2; PGS002738 has covariates listed as "Unknown"; PGS000201/PGS000202 instead report small incremental ΔR2 values (0.01192 and 0.00456), making cross-model comparison uncertain. Additionally, PGS002739 is a direct-match but evaluates only African American/Afro-Caribbean ancestry, so it is not a general replacement for a EUR-evaluated model without a specified deployment ancestry."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001824",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches via trait_efo including bilirubin metabolism disease. PGS001824 and PGS002032 have the closest endpoint wording to the target (phenotyping_reported/trait_reported: Disorders of bilirubin excretion), whereas PGS000924 uses a broader composite endpoint and time-to-event design (disorders of porphyrin and bilirubin metabolism), making it less phenotype-aligned for a generic bilirubin metabolism disease target despite reporting AUROC/R2. Between the two bilirubin-excretion models, visible evidence is essentially tied (same publication family, same training size, same validation size, same partial-r estimate). With no differentiating performance or endpoint details, selection is sub-optimal; PGS001824 is chosen only as a slight preference for the simpler penalized regression model shape (22 variants) given indistinguishable reported partial-r and the lack of PRS-only AUROC/R2 for either."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002092","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target (trait_efo = Dupuytren Contracture; phenotyping_reported is Dupuytren’s disease/contracture wording). However, the set is dominated by pan-trait/portability framework scores rather than a disease-focused multi-cohort model, which limits how strongly one can recommend a single model.

PGS001254 (snpnet; 11 variants) reports explicit discrimination (PGS-only AUROC 0.6646; full-model AUROC 0.7413; incremental AUROC 0.0447) but comes from the UKB "813 traits" sparse snpnet framework—domain policy flags these as needing caution, and the very sparse variant count reinforces that it may not capture enough polygenic signal.

PGS001880 and PGS002092 are sibling models from the UKB "Portability of 245 polygenic scores" framework with similar phenotype and covariates. Both lack AUROC/R² and provide only partial correlation; among them, PGS002092 has slightly higher partial-r (0.0797 vs 0.0783) with the same validation size and ancestry context, and uses LDpred2 (a mildly favorable method tie-break versus penalized regression here). The main limitation is that the performance evidence is incomplete/non-comparable across candidates (partial-r vs AUROC) and all options are framework-derived, so confidence cannot be high."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005271","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates (PGS005270/PGS005271/PGS005272) are direct matches for the target: trait_efo is explicitly Hashimoto’s thyroiditis and phenotyping_reported is lymphocytic thyroiditis (a near-synonym/clinical description consistent with Hashimoto’s). They also come from the same disease-focused multi-biobank publication family with identical validation sample size (n=94,651) and similar multi-ancestry evaluation context, so endpoint fidelity and validation support do not distinguish them strongly.

Given comparable phenotype alignment, the main differentiators are the limited available performance/effect-size signals. PGS005271 shows stronger per-SD effect sizes (OR≈1.55; β≈0.44) than PGS005272 (OR≈1.42; β≈0.35), and it is a genome-wide PRSCS model similar in shape to PGS005272. PGS005270 has a slightly higher reported full-model AUROC (≈0.639) but is a sparse P+T model (55 variants) and the only reported discrimination is full-model AUROC with covariates listed as Unknown, which domain guidance flags as insufficient to treat as PRS-only evidence; the modest AUROC edge is therefore a weak tie-break versus the stronger effect-size evidence favoring PGS005271.

Overall, PGS005271 is the best-supported direct-match among near-clones, but confidence is limited because covariates are Unknown for all candidates and the only AUCs provided appear to be full-model rather than explicitly PRS-only or incremental metrics, reducing comparability and strength of the performance evidence."}
